Yes, Mike, you may cite this additional info as a pers. comm.  Supposedly, Marsh and Hatcher obtained the first bones together.  I suspect one or the other of the ladies must have informed Marsh about their discovery, but, unfortunately, if they did so by either letter or telegram, it is not preserved among the Marsh correspondences at Yale.  However, based on the accession record below, the earliest one that we have for the specimen, I think it was probably Mrs. Ellerman in her capacity as both Postmistress and fossil discoverer who would have reached out to Marsh.

Accession YPM.02054: from YPM.ledger: Rec'd. Nov. 14th, 1889. From Chadron, Nebraska. Three boxes fossils (Freight $7.00) Box 1. Miocene Turtles etc. (4) (one from Bronto. beds.) Box 2. Miocene Rhinoceros skull. (Pine Ridge Agency.) bought of C.H. Little (Farmer.) Box 3. Caudal vertebrae etc. of new Sauropod. col. by J.B.H. & O.C.M, Atlantosaurus beds, S. Dakota, on land Mrs. Rachel Hatch, locality found by Mrs. E.R. Ellerman, Postmistress at Postville, P.O. Sacora Station, S. Dakota. O.C.M. donor. Unpacked Dec. 3, 1889.

Dan

